From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 19:07:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP: do not address Authentication replies to the MLD MAC
 address

An Authentication frame sent to a non-AP MLD was addressed to the MLD MAC
address, leaving it to the driver to map that back to the affiliated STA
that sent the request. The driver can only do so while it still knows the
peer's current link address, and it learns that address only when the STA
is (re)added to it.

A non-AP MLD is free to pick new affiliated STA addresses for every
attempt. IEEE Std 802.11be-2024 12.2.11 requires the addresses to stay
constant only for the duration of an association, and explicitly permits
changing them "when performing association or reassociation to the AP MLD
that it is currently associated". When the peer does that while an
association is still up, the driver's copy is stale, the frame goes out
addressed to an address no STA answers to, and it is never acknowledged.

Per 802.11be-2024 11.3.4.3 the reply belongs to the affiliated STA that
sent the frame with authentication transaction sequence number 1, and an
Authentication frame carries no Link Info (35.3.5.4), so the source
address of the received frame is the only peer link address available.
Use it, which is what handle_assoc() already does for the association
response.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
---

--- a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
+++ b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
@@ -4736,13 +4736,14 @@ static void handle_auth(struct hostapd_d
 	}
 
  fail:
+	/* Addressing the reply to the MLD address relies on the driver mapping
+	 * it back to the link the request came in on, which it can only do
+	 * while it knows that link address. A non-AP MLD picks a fresh link
+	 * address per attempt, so reply to the address that sent the request,
+	 * which is also what handle_assoc() does.
+	 */
 	dst = mgmt->sa;
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_IEEE80211BE
-	if (ap_sta_is_mld(hapd, sta))
-		dst = sta->addr;
-#endif /* CONFIG_IEEE80211BE */
-
 	reply_res = send_auth_reply(hapd, sta, dst, auth_alg,
 				    auth_alg == WLAN_AUTH_SAE ?
 				    auth_transaction : auth_transaction + 1,
